;; spec:   stdlib §5 (nth is the lookup that throws on a bad index, P5), types §2.13
;; expect: trap
;; trap:   nth: index out of range
;; The empty Vec is a variant of its own (an array cannot be made without an
;; element to fill it with), so its nth is a separate arm: index 0 of nothing
;; traps with the same text as every other bad index. A Vec that was filled and
;; whose count is read as zero is not this case: the empty one is made by
;; vec-empty, as `(vec-empty)` in the program says.
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((v: (Vec i64) (vec-empty)))
    (+ (count v) (nth v 0))))
